<!-- Source of truth for the clip-notify Shortcut. run_onchange_after_build-clip-notify
     converts this to a signed .shortcut; importing it is one click, because the
     shortcuts CLI has no create/import verb and the Core Data store behind
     Shortcuts.app is not safe to write.

     `shortcuts run --input-path` hands the shortcut a file rather than a
     string, and the file must be named .txt or the body renders empty --
     that is what clip-receive's temp file extension is for. The Text action
     coerces the input before the notification reads it, which is belt and
     braces: a body referencing Shortcut Input directly was measured to work
     too, but only for text input. -->

